{
  "set": "djfilter",
  "description": "m39-i, the .djFilter insert: ONE bipolar knob, which is the whole instrument. Every variant plays the IDENTICAL sustained saw-pad chord (8 bars at 120 BPM) so nothing but the filter differs.\n\nTHE EFFECT IS A MOTION, NOT A SETTING, so every filtered variant is an automated SWEEP on `position` rather than a parked value \u2014 out from the centre to the extreme at bar 5, and back to the centre by bar 9. A DJ filter held still is a tone control.\n\nNO VARIANT DIFFERS ON TWO THINGS AT ONCE, and the axes are nested rather than mixed: variants 1 -> 2 -> 3 move on POSITION (none / sweep down / sweep up), and variants 2 -> 4 move on RESONANCE alone \u2014 variant 4 is variant 2's sweep, byte-for-byte in every other parameter, with resonance raised from 0.2 to 0.9.\n\nWHAT TO LISTEN FOR. Variant 1 is the pad untouched, so you know what is being taken away. In 'sweep-down-lp' the knob turns POSITIVE and a low-pass falls from 20 kHz down to about 56 Hz: the BRIGHTNESS leaves first and the chord goes muffled, underwater, then comes back. In 'sweep-up-hp' the knob turns NEGATIVE and a high-pass climbs from 20 Hz up to about 7 kHz: the WEIGHT leaves first and the chord goes thin and radio-like before filling back in. In 'high-resonance' the same downward sweep grows a whistle that rides along just above the cutoff \u2014 the singing edge that is what people mean by a DJ filter.\n\nWHAT WOULD MAKE THIS INAUDIBLE: nothing on a normal system \u2014 this axis removes whole halves of the spectrum. But 'high-resonance' is measurably QUIETER than 'sweep-down-lp' and that is deliberate, not a mistake: the resonant section is scaled by sqrt(0.707/Q) so a Q-10 peak cannot slam the mix, which costs about 11.5 dB of passband at full resonance. If you judge that variant purely on level you will pick the wrong one; listen for the whistle, not the loudness.\n\nERRATA E4, RULED 2026-09-19: the knob direction was REVERSED after this set was first written, to match the hardware it imitates \u2014 a club mixer's filter knob is low-pass to the LEFT and high-pass to the RIGHT, and the frozen contract had it inverted with no rationale. The variant NAMES and the SOUNDS they describe are unchanged; the signed `position` values behind them were swapped instead, so 'sweep-down-lp' is still the muffled one.",
